{:edb/db-before-t 1631 :edb/db-after-t 1632 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534944 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:40:42.547Z" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147331 1100 "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147331 1100 "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147331 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{3/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147331 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147332 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147332 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{x^2}{y}$." 13194139534944 false] [17592186147332 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z =2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\frac{2}{x}, Q(x) = 2x^{2}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{∫(2/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}z) &= x^{2} \\cdot (2x^{2}) \\\\\nx^{2} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} + 2xz &= 2x^{4} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2}) &= 2x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2})dx &= ∫2x^{4}dx \\\\\nzx^{2} &= \\frac{2x^{5}}{5} + C_{1} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2x^{5} + C}{5x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1$.\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}$. This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}\n$$" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147332 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147334 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147334 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n2. $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n3. $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147334 1145 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = e^x y^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = -xy^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147334 1145 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321499 13194139534944 true] [17592186147392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321541 13194139534944 true] [17592186147392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321548 13194139534944 true] [17592186147393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321555 13194139534944 true] [17592186147393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321562 13194139534944 true] [17592186147393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321569 13194139534944 true] [17592186147393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321576 13194139534944 true] [17592186147395 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321506 13194139534944 true] [17592186147395 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321513 13194139534944 true] [17592186147395 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321520 13194139534944 true] [17592186147395 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321527 13194139534944 true] [17592186147395 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321534 13194139534944 true] [17592186147666 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534944 true] [17592186147666 1100 "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + x^{2}y = xy^{4}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147666 1100 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147666 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534944 true] [17592186147667 1100 "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{y}{x} = x\\sqrt{y}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?" 13194139534944 false] [17592186147667 1100 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147667 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147668 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534944 true] [17592186147668 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186147668 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - y = y^{2}e^{x}$." 13194139534944 false] [17592186147668 1145 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321500 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1089 "q-106071" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1100 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2})=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1113 #uuid "81e61808-2e93-5c92-87e6-c51f5a422b87" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321499 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy & =3{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}y & =3{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=3{x}^{-2},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-2}=3{x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z=3{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{-1}z=-6{x}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-6{x}^{-2}.$" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321501 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321502 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321503 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321504 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321505 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1041 "selection" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321501 13194139534944 true] [17592186321500 1110 #uuid "c54109d3-e3be-5884-906e-34b15ffdbf65" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321501 1034 "-6{x}^{-2}" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534944 true] [17592186321501 1111 #uuid "3e7eb463-209e-5803-8685-e5a512aeba24" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321502 1034 "2{x}^{-2}" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534944 true] [17592186321502 1111 #uuid "9f3820dc-6520-57a7-9280-7d5fefc81d71" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321503 1034 "-3{x}^{-1}" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534944 true] [17592186321503 1111 #uuid "7230d4ca-3e31-58e4-a7bf-3b41dbfc0d98" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321504 1034 "4{x}^{-1}" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534944 true] [17592186321504 1111 #uuid "9222a794-6bbc-523f-bd20-00c12a22dc00" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321505 1034 "-6{x}^{-1}" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534944 true] [17592186321505 1111 #uuid "c6dfe1ff-a514-5308-95c8-2bf5dcd15898" 13194139534944 true] [17592186321506 10